 reasoning  sometimes to figure out what's in your  self-interest and and you know the best  way to go about getting the shortest  means to the ends that you want but  that's not a serious or philosophical  problem philosophical problems arise  when something that's in your  self-interest isn't in someone else's  and you you need to weigh things I mean  the basic answer to the question is  morality ultimately selfish is no but  what I've been thinking about since the  pandemic started is actually that the  entire very long discussion about  self-interest and the common good is  actually being undermined in a quite  promising and beautiful way and one  example of course is just what we're all  being told about wearing masks we're  wearing masks not to protect ourselves  but to protect other people and if they  wear masks it will protect us I mean  that's just a rock bottom level and a  perhaps a paradigm but also what you  mentioned at the beginning our  realization that not only our grocery  store workers and trash collectors and  you know nurses the most vulnerable and  usually the worst paid there are also  the most important all of us who get to  stay at home and work online and jabber  and conversations like this source file  our stories are non-essential the world  will go on turning without us and I  think there's been enough consciousness  of that and discussion of that all over  the world that people are beginning to  ask  it is not an act of generosity to make  sure that those people who keep the woo  keep our world's going are given the  same kinds of we're better working  conditions pay status and respect than  they have hitherto received because the  world will not go on turning if it  doesn't happen that way thank you very  much  Patricia can I ask each one's the same  question well I'll give it a shot and  I'm going to come at it from a different  direction I'm going to start with the  question that Darwin asked in the  descent of man in 1871 and Darwin knew  very well of course that mammals and  birds generally are intensely social the  doorman said where does our moral sense  or our conscience come from and he  suggested that it had three components  one is instinct one is learning and the  third is what we might call  problem-solving or reasoning now by and  large evolutionary biologists have been  disinclined to thinking that instinct  could be any part of the story because  they think gee you know if some person  had the instinct to be altruistic then  the other guys would take advantage of  them he wouldn't be able to reproduce  and it would be wiped out but it turned  out that there is an interesting story  about why mammals and birds are social  in the way that they are so about 200  million years ago warm-blooded animals  appeared on the planet now this was a  tremendous ecological advantage because  they can forage at night and they could  forage in cooler places but it came with  a huge cost gram for gram a warm-blooded  animal has to eat ten times as much so  in order to accommodate that those  animals warm-blooded were favored if  they were smart and the smarter the  better  now there's really two ways to get smart  one is to build it all in the jeans but  that's an extremely long and slow  process the other way is to learn and  the bigger the learning the smarter you  are and so what really happened then was  that big learning took place but the  downside the cost of big learning is  because the brain has to build structure  every time you learn anything infants  have to be born very immature and that  means they are very vulnerable so those  animals were favored where the parents  are in particular the mother formed  strong bonds to the infant and cared for  the infant as though it was an extension  of herself that she took care of its  food its warmth and safety now it wasn't  because it was fun it was because she  was wired to do this and we now know  it's something about the neural  chemicals that are involved in that and  they have to do with oxytocin and neural  chemicals that make you feel really good  the endocannabinoids now as species  traveled the planet and found additional  niches different manners and ways of  being social emerged some animals found  it very convenient to live in fairly  large groups some animals found that  para bond long term pair bonding was an  ecological advantage others didn't have  pair bonding but there was bonding  between kin bonding between friends and  so forth so if I can put it in a very  simple way but first I should make one  clarification by a moral decision here  I'm going to mean a decision in which I  am cursed in order to benefit another so  in the case of many social animals what  we see is that  there is significant caring for others  it says though to put it in a very  highly simplified way and I'm sure you  know I'll get beat up for this it's as  though bonding baguettes caring and  caring baguettes morality and we see  aspects of caring for others in the all  of the intensely social animals such as  wolves elephants baboons and certainly  also in humans now the learning part of  what Darwin kicked up on involves the  reward system so very early on the  infant's learn how to navigate their  social world but their reward system is  deeply involved so that when they  produce a behavior that is rewarded they  feel good they get a dopamine hit when  they produce behavior for which they are  scolded they get a serotonin hit to put  it simply and so it does mean and and  and I know some contents think that this  is horrifying it does mean that we often  take real pleasure in doing the right  thing in doing the good thing we derive  satisfaction and joy and pleasure from  doing that we also know that if you are  a social animal and you behave in these  socially caring ways that it's good not  only for your mental health it's also  very good for your physical health  it doesn't precisely imply anything  about policy for that I think we have to  figure out things in the way we always  have we have to come together compromise  make decisions do the best we can and by  and large with the exception of  Psychopaths  people tend to want to do that that they  are they have common sense basically  they are decent and when there is an  emergency people respond very well they  do respond in different  weights because we are different from  one another and that reflects our  cultural background but it also reflects  certain deep innate character illogical  features such as whether we are very  inclined to be rule-bound or whether we  are inclined to be rule-breakers and  there's really important data about that  but this is just by way of saying that  the mix is very complex and there is no  simple rule that tells us what the right  thing to do is okay thank you very much  and now finally I turn to 2 Peter  morality seems to me to be tested most  of all in the overcoming of fear to  continue watching this video click the  link in the top left or in the  description below or visit a IDOT TV for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas

